        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "095623bc-c1d8-5804-9a27-80541587028d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Christopher I of Denmark, Eric IV of Denmark, Abel of Denmark, Margaret Sambiria, Eric V of Denmark, Queen Sophie\n- Places: Nykøbing Castle\n- Roles: king of Denmark, Queen consort, regent, queen dowager\n\nFacts:\n- Eric IV of Denmark, also known as Eric Ploughpenny or Eric Plowpenny and in Danish as Erik Plovpenning, was born around 1216 and died on 10 August 1250.\n- Eric IV of Denmark was king of Denmark from 1241 until his death in 1250.\n- Christopher I of Denmark succeeded his brothers Eric IV Plovpenning and Abel of Denmark on the Danish throne.\n- Margaret Sambiria, also called Margrethe Sambiria or Sambirsdatter in Danish, lived from about 1230 to 1282.\n- Margaret Sambiria was the Queen consort of Christopher I of Denmark and later acted as regent for Eric V of Denmark.\n- Nykøbing Castle was a royal palace and the traditional residence of Denmark's queen dowagers.\n- Nykøbing Castle included Margrethe Sambiria and Queen Sophie among its resident queen dowagers.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "06e7b388-25dc-53b3-a376-1c455befe588",
        "text": "Page: Christopher I of Denmark. Sentence: He succeeded his brothers Eric IV Plovpenning and Abel of Denmark on the throne.\nPage: Eric IV of Denmark. Sentence: Eric IV, also known as Eric Ploughpenny or Eric Plowpenny (Danish: \"Erik Plovpenning\" ), (  1216 – 10 August 1250) was king of Denmark from 1241 until his death in 1250.\nPage: Margaret Sambiria. Sentence: Margaret Sambiria, in Danish: \"Margrethe Sambiria\" or \"Sambirsdatter,\" (1230?\nPage: Margaret Sambiria. Sentence: –1282) was the Queen consort of Christopher I of Denmark, and acted as regent for Eric V of Denmark.\nPage: Nykøbing Castle. Sentence: A royal palace, it was the traditional residence of Denmark's queen dowagers including Margrethe Sambiria (1230?\nPage: Nykøbing Castle. Sentence: –1282) and Queen Sophie (1557–1631).",
